WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.710
Speaker 1: We have news in terms of the debt
ceiling.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.899
I'm going to give you the news, but I'm also
going to tell you why I really haven't been

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:11.720
covering the debt ceiling story that much.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.020
And I know that there's some in the audience
who agree with my reasoning, and there's some

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.169
in the audience who who don't.

00:00:17.169 --> 00:00:22.900
So to begin with, the House is going to vote
to extend the debt ceiling through early December.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.679
CNN reporting on this The House of Representatives
expected to vote today to extend the debt

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:32.660
limit through early December after the Senate
approved a stopgap measure last week in a

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.590
bid to avert a catastrophic default and economic
disaster.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:43.100
Once the Democratic controlled House passes
the short term extension, it will be cleared

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.800
for President Joe Biden's signature.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.490
Janet Yellen, the Treasury secretary, warned
lawmakers the federal government will likely

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.420
run out of cash by October 18 unless Congress
raises the debt ceiling, setting up a ticking

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.590
clock and high stakes.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.790
Congress may not even have that long to act,
since the deadline is more of a best guess

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.949
estimate than a set in stone deadline.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.600
That dynamic intensified pressure on Democrats
and Republicans to reach a deal to address

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.940
the debt limit.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.920
OK, so what we're talking about here
Speaker 2: is

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.780
Speaker 1: passing a debt limit increase to
enable the Treasury to keep borrowing money

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.540
to pay bills for two months.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.460
This would be temporary.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.939
This will set up another round of the exact
same thing in December.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:30.560
So why haven't I been talking about this that
much?

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.340
Quite frankly, and I want to be careful with
my language.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.689
You as an individual should barely care about
this.

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:43.579
The debt ceiling was created in the first
place to put a limit on how much debt the

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:48.590
government could take on without requiring
a really clumsy process that was in place

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:49.590
beforehand.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.820
We don't even need to go over the process
that was in place beforehand.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:58.359
The point was just let them take on debt,
but cap it so they can't go beyond a certain

00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:00.000
amount without coming back to us.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.369
It's sort of like, actually, he'll.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:06.429
Bloom has a good Twitter thread on this and
say, he'll tweeted, This is sort of like it

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:10.840
used to be that you had to get approval every
time you wanted to buy something on your credit

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.840
card.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.349
Now it's you just have a spending limit in
total on the credit card and you can buy whatever

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:17.790
you want until you hit that limit.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.099
If you hit the limit, you got to ask for an
increase.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:22.530
Every once in a while, you're going to need
to raise it.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.530
Why?

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.129
Well, the country keeps growing, the economy
keeps growing.

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:32.030
And so even if you maintain the same percentage
of the economy and debt, the raw numbers are

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.180
going to go up over time.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.489
But this entire process was basically a rubber
stamp.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:38.489
We've got to raise it.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:39.489
We've got to raise it.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:40.489
We're a growing country.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:41.489
Our economy grows.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:42.629
We've got to raise the debt ceiling.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:50.709
Debt ceiling increases, remember, are needed
for prior spending that's already been authorized.

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:55.440
Republicans like to play this game where they
say, we're not going to do this and allow

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.390
Democrats to keep spending us into debt oblivion.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:02:59.650
Hold on.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.140
That's grandstanding.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.750
That's a lie it.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.670
They want to pretend that this is to stop
Democrats from spending.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.209
It's for prior spending that was already approved
by Congress.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:16.130
The fear is, if they don't do this, the U.S.
will default on its debt so that all the fear

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.299
mongering and the headline start.

00:03:18.299 --> 00:03:22.380
And Republicans say we're going to default
because we have too much debt and Democrats

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.480
won't stop spending.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:30.140
The reality is we default if Republicans don't
raise the debt ceiling, which is supposed

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.269
to be merely perfunctory.

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:34.629
Some Republicans will say that's the problem.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:39.390
It can't be perfunctory that we go into more
and more and more debt.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:44.960
But the time to talk about that is when you
authorize the spending that now simply needs

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.129
to be paid for.

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:51.959
And what we end up doing is giving oxygen
to something where nobody really benefits

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:52.959
from there.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.730
Being more stories about the debt ceiling,
we're actually giving them attention, which

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.580
makes them more eager not to just raise the
debt ceiling.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:02.890
And let's continue.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:05.690
So they're going to get this solved for two
months.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.810
It'll come up again in December.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:13.060
It'll get solved again, and it's in no one's
interest to actually not raise the debt ceiling

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.349
over the long term.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:19.769
And if people didn't ignorantly react to it,
it would get less oxygen oxygen to begin with,

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:24.430
and it would deflate this as something that
Republicans want to get attention for.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:29.420
Think about the number of interviews over
the last, you know, weeks, months, however

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:35.300
long all about this and in the interviews,
they're not even mentioning the fundamental

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:36.690
point, which is.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.310
We are now Republicans.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:44.220
We are now trying not to raise the debt ceiling
to pay for spending we already approved.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.300
We already approved the spending.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.310
It's not for new spending, it's not for Joe
Biden's new proposals, it's just not what

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:49.310
it's for.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:53.990
So the reason I haven't been covering it is
this really shouldn't be a thing in that this

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:56.190
is just a perfunctory part of running government.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.550
You want to run government a different way
where this doesn't come up.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.780
We could talk about that.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.430
You want to have a bigger conversation about
debt and deficit.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.430
We can do that.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.290
But this is for already approved spending.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.930
You can't now say, Well, we approved it, but
we're not going to cut the check, so to speak,

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.330
or allow the spending to happen to cover,
allow the debt ceiling to increase, to cover

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.810
the spending.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.120
That's why I haven't been covering it.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.600
I don't think any of us really benefit from
talking endlessly about this.

